      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine how many books Melvin will have left over after repacking them into boxes with 40 books each. Here are the steps involved:\n\n1. **Calculate the total number of books in the warehouse:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of books} = \\text{Number of boxes} \\times \\text{Books per box}\n   \\]\n   Given:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of boxes} = 1335\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Books per box} = 39\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of books} = 1335 \\times 39\n   \\]\n\n2. **Perform the multiplication:**\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 39 = 1335 \\times (40 - 1) = 1335 \\times 40 - 1335 \\times 1\n   \\]\n   Calculate \\(1335 \\times 40\\):\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 40 = 53400\n   \\]\n   Calculate \\(1335 \\times 1\\):\n   \\[\n   1335 \\times 1 = 1335\n   \\]\n   Subtract the two results:\n   \\[\n   53400 - 1335 = 52065\n   \\]\n   So, the total number of books is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of books} = 52065\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine how many books are left over after repacking:**\n   We need to find how many books are left when repacking 52065 books into boxes with 40 books each:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of full boxes} = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{52065}{40} \\right\\rfloor\n   \\]\n   Perform the division:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{52065}{40} = 1301.625\n   \\]\n   The integer part of the division gives the number of full boxes:\n   \\[\n   \\left\\lfloor 1301.625 \\right\\rfloor = 1301\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the number of leftover books:**\n   The number of leftover books is the remainder of the division:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Leftover books} = 52065 - (1301 \\times 40)\n   \\]\n   Calculate \\(1301 \\times 40\\):\n   \\[\n   1301 \\times 40 = 52040\n   \\]\n   Subtract this from the total number of books:\n   \\[\n   52065 - 52040 = 25\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of books left over after repacking is \\(\\boxed{25}\\).",
